# Emergency deploy path: the image that is already running, plus this tree's code.
#
# Why this exists. A clean `Dockerfile` build produces a fresh ~250 MB (compressed)
# venv layer that has to be uploaded. This uplink cannot complete a single upload
# much above 25 MB -- measured with plain curl against Cloudflare, no Docker in
# the path: 5/15/25 MB succeed, 50 MB and 100 MB die mid-body. So a normal deploy
# cannot finish from here, however many times it is retried.
#
# Every layer of an image already in Cloudflare's registry is, by definition,
# already in Cloudflare's registry. Building on top of the deployed image and
# copying only `src/` and `deploy/` over it makes the push a few hundred kilobytes
# instead of a few hundred megabytes. The venv, base image, user, environment and
# CMD are inherited unchanged, so the result is the running production image with
# new code on it -- not a different environment.
#
# Usage:
#   1. `npx wrangler containers images list` -> newest tag
#   2. set BASE below to it
#   3. point wrangler.jsonc containers[].image at this file
#   4. `npx wrangler deploy`
#   5. put wrangler.jsonc back
#
# Verify before you use it that no file has been DELETED from src/ since the base
# image was built -- COPY overwrites and adds, it does not remove, so a deleted
# module would linger:
#   docker run --rm --entrypoint sh $BASE -c 'cd /app && find src deploy -type f'
#
# The clean `Dockerfile` remains the normal path. Use this when the network will
# not carry a full push, and rebuild properly from a better connection.
ARG BASE=registry.cloudflare.com/0164f94eae0d65c54aab03c444d3aba1/signal-basin-geysercontainer@sha256:ce363c6bbbbd857e1b97bf7334b11320bb372b6485113b8b6b733e31a3ec9914
FROM ${BASE}

COPY --chown=geyser:geyser src /app/src
COPY --chown=geyser:geyser deploy /app/deploy
